### How do you invert a one-to-many map?

The source map has departments as keys and lists of names as values. You stream entrySet, and for each entry you expand the name list while remembering the department. Then you collect the pairs into the inverted map.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class InvertMap {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, List<String>> deptToNames = Map.of(
            "Loans",   List.of("Rajesh", "Amit"),
            "IT",      List.of("Priya", "Kavita"),
            "Finance", List.of("Neha")
        );

        Map<String, String> nameToDept = deptToNames.entrySet().stream()
            .flatMap(entry -> entry.getValue().stream()
                .map(name -> Map.entry(name, entry.getKey())))
            .collect(Collectors.toMap(
                Map.Entry::getKey,
                Map.Entry::getValue));

        System.out.println(nameToDept);
        System.out.println("Priya works in: " + nameToDept.get("Priya"));
    }
}
```

```text
{Neha=Finance, Kavita=IT, Rajesh=Loans, Amit=Loans, Priya=IT}
Priya works in: IT
```

### Why is flatMap needed here?

Each entry has a list of names. Without flatMap you would have a stream of lists. flatMap flattens those nested lists into a single stream of individual name-department pairs.

```text
  Before flatMap:  Stream<Entry<String, List<String>>>
    {Loans: [Rajesh, Amit]}, {IT: [Priya, Kavita]}

  After flatMap:   Stream<Entry<String, String>>
    {Rajesh, Loans}, {Amit, Loans}, {Priya, IT}, {Kavita, IT}
```

### What happens if a name appears in two departments?

The two-argument toMap throws IllegalStateException on a duplicate key.

```java
Map<String, List<String>> withDuplicate = Map.of(
    "Loans", List.of("Rajesh", "Amit"),
    "IT",    List.of("Amit", "Kavita")  // Amit appears twice!
);

// WRONG: throws IllegalStateException for key "Amit"
Map<String, String> crash = withDuplicate.entrySet().stream()
    .flatMap(e -> e.getValue().stream()
        .map(name -> Map.entry(name, e.getKey())))
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Map.Entry::getKey, Map.Entry::getValue));
```

To handle duplicates, either supply a merge function or invert into a list:

```java
// Option 1: merge function (keep the first department)
Map<String, String> keepFirst = withDuplicate.entrySet().stream()
    .flatMap(e -> e.getValue().stream()
        .map(name -> Map.entry(name, e.getKey())))
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Map.Entry::getKey,
        Map.Entry::getValue,
        (existing, incoming) -> existing));

// Option 2: invert into Map<String, List<String>>
Map<String, List<String>> nameToAllDepts = withDuplicate.entrySet().stream()
    .flatMap(e -> e.getValue().stream()
        .map(name -> Map.entry(name, e.getKey())))
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        Map.Entry::getKey,
        Collectors.mapping(Map.Entry::getValue, Collectors.toList())));
System.out.println(nameToAllDepts);
// {Amit=[Loans, IT], Rajesh=[Loans], Kavita=[IT]}
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: forgetting to handle the uniqueness assumption.

```java
// WRONG: assumes all names are unique across departments
// but does not document or enforce that assumption
Map<String, String> fragile = deptToNames.entrySet().stream()
    .flatMap(e -> e.getValue().stream()
        .map(n -> Map.entry(n, e.getKey())))
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Map.Entry::getKey, Map.Entry::getValue));

// CORRECT: make the assumption explicit with a merge function
Map<String, String> robust = deptToNames.entrySet().stream()
    .flatMap(e -> e.getValue().stream()
        .map(n -> Map.entry(n, e.getKey())))
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Map.Entry::getKey,
        Map.Entry::getValue,
        (a, b) -> { throw new IllegalStateException(
            "Duplicate name: " + a + " vs " + b); }));
```

### Can you invert a one-to-one map more simply?

If the source map is one-to-one (each value is a single string, not a list), no flatMap is needed.

```java
Map<String, String> deptToManager = Map.of(
    "Loans", "Rajesh", "IT", "Priya", "Finance", "Neha");

Map<String, String> managerToDept = deptToManager.entrySet().stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Map.Entry::getValue,
        Map.Entry::getKey));
System.out.println(managerToDept);
// {Rajesh=Loans, Priya=IT, Neha=Finance}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Inversion safety depends on value cardinality: a one-to-many map inverts to many-to-one only if the many side is unique.
- When uniqueness is not guaranteed, groupingBy is the safer inverse, producing Map<String, List<String>>.
- Map.entry() creates an immutable entry; AbstractMap.SimpleEntry is the mutable alternative if you need to modify it.
- Being explicit about the uniqueness assumption is what separates a robust inversion from a fragile one.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you invert a Map<String, Set<String>>?" Same pattern, just stream the set instead of the list.
- "What if the original map has null values?" Filter out null entries before flatMapping.
- "Can you invert a Map<String, Integer> where values repeat?" Use groupingBy to collect keys sharing a value into a list.
